{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Scottsboro homes have downspout problems they don't know about. Older homes in town often have the original, undersized downspouts — adequate when installed, but not enough for today's more intense storm patterns. Lakeside properties frequently have downspouts that simply dump water onto sloped lots, sending it right back toward the foundation.</p>

<p>When downspouts can't keep up, water backs up in gutters and overflows. Even worse, many downspouts deposit water right next to the foundation. On Scottsboro's clay soil, that water doesn't absorb — it pools and eventually finds its way into basements and crawlspaces.</p>

<p>The fix is usually straightforward: properly sized downspouts positioned and extended to move water well away from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective extensions.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and local rainfall. North Alabama's 55+ inches of annual rain means we often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows — at corners and low points in your gutter system. Extensions (or better yet, underground drains) then carry that water well away from your foundation.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been solving drainage problems in Jackson County for over 20 years. We understand Scottsboro's soil conditions, the unique needs of lakeside properties, and how to work with older downtown homes.</p>

<p>From simple downspout extensions to complete system redesigns, we'll help you find the right solution for your home and budget.</p>
